---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import FormBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/FormBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="form-fieldset-has">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code> are the right
      markup for a group of fields, but they come with a border, padding and a legend
      notched into the border.
    </p>
    <p>
      The spacing also depends on what is inside: a description under the
      legend, a list of checkboxes, a row of buttons, a required field. That
      usually means modifier classes or props kept in sync with the content.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>all: unset</code> on both drops the native box and the notch. What's
        left is a grid
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>legend:has(+ .description)</code> moves the spacing from the legend
        to the description
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:has(&gt; .checkbox):not(:has(&gt; :not(.checkbox)))</code>
        matches a group of only checkboxes, radios or switches, and tightens the gap
      </li>
      <li>The same pattern turns a group of only buttons into a row</li>
      <li>
        <code>fieldset:has(:required)</code> adds an asterisk to the legend
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.fieldset {
  all: unset;
  display: grid;
  gap: 0.25rem;
  min-inline-size: 0;
}

.fieldset legend:has(+ .description) {
  margin-block-end: 0;
}

.group:has(> .checkbox):not(:has(> :not(.checkbox))) {
  gap: 0.5rem;
}

.fieldset:has(:required) legend::after {
  color: var(--field-required-color);
  content: "*";
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <FormBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>:has()</code> and <code>all</code> are widely available
      </li>
      <li>
        Each <code>:has()</code> rule stands alone, so a browser without it drops
        only those rules: the legend keeps its margin, groups keep the default gap,
        buttons stack and the asterisk is gone
      </li>
      <li>
        The <code>required</code> attribute still tells assistive tech, the asterisk
        is only visual
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
